WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.050
Here's the first thing that might surprise
audiences about Hard Kill: The low-budget

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.990
action drama is low-key killing it with a
stronger-than-expected showing on Netflix.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.910
Leave it to the streaming masses to show the
film critics of the world that they just don't

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.910
understand fun.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.210
If you were given the basic plot summary –– a
hardened ex-special forces mercenary is hired

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.359
by a tech billionaire to save his kidnapped
daughter and safeguard a potentially world-shattering

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:25.310
piece of MacGuffin software –– which one
would you guess Bruce Willis was playing?

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.349
Okay, so he's not the kidnapped daughter,
but neither is he the mercenary in this instance.

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:34.430
That part goes to Jesse Metcalfe, 23 years
younger than Willis and more tolerant of the

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.310
hits and punishment that come with being an
action star.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.140
That leaves Willis as Donovan Chalmers, corporate
CEO and scarf enthusiast, capable of running

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.280
a company that delves vaguely into AI and
quantum computing.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.330
Does that sound complicated and perhaps a
little sci-fi?

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.790
It's really neither.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.600
Hard Kill is about as uncomplicated an action
movie as you can find on Netflix, which is

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.140
why its chart-topping dominance comes as a
bit of a surprise.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.520
In Hard Kill, written by Chris LaMont and
Joe Russo (not the Joe Russo you're thinking

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.300
of), Willis' Chalmers and Metcalfe's Derek
Miller team up to rescue his daughter and

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.890
the software from the hands of a terrorist
mastermind whose nom de guerre is "The Pardoner."

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.640
Willis is certainly the film's big above-the-title
star.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.780
While there's no escaping the title intentionally
reminiscent of Die Hard, the movie is actually

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.110
it's own thing entirely.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.850
Amazingly, this is Willis' third collaboration
with director Matt Eskandari over the last

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:27.660
two years, after 2019's Trauma Center and
2020's Survive the Night.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.520
None of these cinematic outings have exactly
dazzled, though Survive the Night was arguably

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.600
the best received out of the trio.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.210
Hard Kill presents a lot more of the same
from this thrifty and fecund partnership,

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.230
so if you liked the first two, chances are
good you'll find something to enjoy in the

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.230
third.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.220
Metcalfe is a veteran of primetime soaps who's
carved out a second career as a low-budget

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.690
action and horror hero.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:51.220
He played gardener-turned-lover John Rowland
on ABC's Lynchian suburban gothic Desperate

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:56.990
Housewives and grieving-parent-turned-vengeful-punisher
Craig Landry in 2010's The Tortured.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:00.710
He starred as Christopher Ewing on the reboot
of Dallas and as Chase Carter in two movie

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.500
adaptations of the Dead Rising video game
series.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.800
He was Trace Riley in Chesapeake Shores and
Luke Graves in Escape Plan 2: Hades.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.250
You get the idea.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:14.530
Along for the ride are Lala Kent as Chalmers'
kidnapped daughter, Eva, and WWE star Eva

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.170
Marie as Miller's team member Sasha Zindel.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:22.050
Actors Texas Battle and Sergio Rizzuto return
from Trauma Center to play ally Nick Fox and

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.390
the villainous Pardoner, respectively.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.430
Hard Kill is unlikely to win any critics'
choice awards this year.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:32.280
It has a zero-percent rating from just 18
reviews on Rotten Tomatoes, with most of those

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.950
reporting that Willis' performance is somewhere
on a spectrum from exhausted to disdainful.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.060
Frank Scheck of The Hollywood Reporter said,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.090
"The production notes inform us that the unimaginatively
titled Hard Kill was filmed in a mere ten

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.900
days, making you wonder how they spent eight
of them."

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.700
The Guardian's Mike McCahill wrote in a one-star
review,

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.600
"Look beyond the lifelessly choreographed
shootouts,and you keep catching glimpses of

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.191
ghosts: those of American industry, yes, but
also those of the American action movie, once

00:02:58.191 --> 00:03:03.160
manufactured with a skill, verve and wit wholly
absent from these painfully long 98 minutes

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.190
... Anyone hoping Bruce Willis might enjoy
a career renaissance after his rediscovery

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:11.470
in M. Night Shyamalan's Split and Glass: lower
those expectations now."

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:15.560
Of course, there's a certain niche a movie
like this is trying to hit, and close examination

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.280
for the purpose of writing a review isn't
quite it.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.130
While the film has undoubtedly withered under
the critical gaze, it's actually above water

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.250
with the fans.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.090
For what it's trying to be, Hard Kill appears
to be more successful than the scathing reviews

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.090
let on.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:32.819
It holds an audience score of 67 percent on
Rotten Tomatoes, and judging by how it's doing

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:36.030
on Netflix, lots of people are willing to
at least give it a shot.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.110
You could be the next convert.

